<p> (4&#160;May 1726&#160;– 14&#160;July 1778) was a  officer and politician. He saw service in the English Channel and Mediterranean Sea during the . At the start of the , he was promoted to captain and given a command on the . There he played a major part in the , and fought against the French ship . Suckling was employed in the aftermath of the  to destroy French fortifications on the  and went on  at the end of the war. He was given his next command during the  in 1770, and took his nephew  with him. In 1775, Suckling was appointed as the  by , the . Suckling oversaw the Royal Navy's mobilisation when the  began. In 1776, he was also elected member of Parliament for . He died unexpectedly in 1778. 
