<p> was a powerful  in 2015 and one of the strongest hurricanes on record to affect the Bahamas. It evolved from a  to a , becoming a Category&#160;4 major hurricane on the  on October&#160;1. On October&#160;3, Joaquin acquired  of 155&#160;mph (250&#160;km/h). Between October&#160;1 and 3, it caused extensive damage on , , , , and . Prolonged, intense winds brought down trees and power lines, and unroofed homes. Flooding persisted for days after the hurricane's departure. Relief efforts were hampered by heavy damage to airstrips and flooded roads. The , Cuba, and Haiti were all severely affected. Over the Southeastern United States,  drew tremendous moisture from the hurricane, leading to catastrophic flooding in . Over its lifetime, Joaquin killed 34 people and caused US$120&#160;million in damage. 
