<p>The  is a  bronze sculptural group of  dating to the 2nd century AD. A miniature of the cult statue that stood in the , Lebanon, it depicts the god as a beardless youth wearing a , a basket-shaped headdress, and an , a close-fitting dress, under ornate armor. The front of the armor bears busts of seven deities associated with celestial bodies—, , , , ,  (replacing ), and —arranged in an order encoding both the Chaldean sequence of planets and the days of the . The piece illustrates the  of Canaanite, Greek, and Roman traditions, tracing the evolution of Heliopolitan Jupiter from the Canaanite storm god  into a cosmic deity of planetary order and prophecy. Named after its former owner, the Beiruti aristocrat , and acquired by the  in 1939, the piece inaugurated the first issue of , the leading French journal of Levantine archaeology, in 1920. 
