<p>The  is a poorly studied volcano in the Range&#160;2 Coast Land District of , Canada. It lies within the  of the  and reaches an elevation of 2,860 metres (9,380 feet), although some sources give an elevation as high as 3,160&#160;m (10,370&#160;ft). Deeply eroded, the caldera is about 25 by 20 kilometres (16 by 12 miles) in size and has a rugged topography. The area is the origin of several streams and contains several named , including . Volcanic rocks deposited by eruptions include , ,  and . They are exposed in valleys, but at higher elevations they are largely buried under glacial ice. The Silverthrone Caldera was a source of  for indigenous peoples during the . Geological studies have been conducted at the volcano since at least the 1960s, but its very remote location has impeded detailed fieldwork. 
